/** @page qore_functions Functions

    @tableofcontents

    @section function_declarations Function Declarations
    A function is declared in %Qore by using the keyword <tt><b>sub</b></tt> (for subroutine) as follows.

    @par Function Declaration Syntax
    <tt>[@ref mod_public "public"] [@ref synchronized "synchronized"] [@ref deprecated "deprecated"]</tt> <em>@ref data_type_declarations "[return_type]"</em> <tt><b>sub</b></tt> <em>function_name</em><tt>(</tt><em>@ref data_type_declarations "[param_type]"</em> <em>var_name</em> ...<tt>])</tt> <em>@ref function_domains "[[dom=domain,...]]"</em> <tt>{</tt>\n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>@ref statements "statement(s)..."</em>\n
    }\n\n
    or the deprecated alternate syntax with the <tt><b>returns</b></tt> keyword:\n
    <tt>[@ref mod_public "public"] [@ref synchronized "synchronized"] [@ref deprecated "deprecated"]</tt> <tt><b>sub</b></tt> <em>function_name</em><tt>(</tt><em>@ref data_type_declarations "[param_type]"</em> <em>var_name</em> ...<tt>]) \b returns</tt> <em>@ref data_type_declarations "return_type"</em> <tt>{</tt>\n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>@ref statements "statement(s)..."</em>\n
    }\n

    Function names must be valid %Qore identifiers.

    When defining a @ref user_modules "user module", the function definition can be preceded by
    @ref mod_public "public", which means that the function variant will be available (imported) in the
    @ref Qore::Program "Program" object importing the module.  See @ref mod_public "public" for more information.

    @subsection function_parameters Function Parameters
    Variables listed in parentheses after the function name are the parameters to the function and automatically get
    local lexical scoping.

    Type declarations optionally precede the parameter variable and will restrict any arguments passed to the type
    declared. The same function can be declared multiple times if each declaration has different parameter types; this
    is called @ref overloading "overloading" the function.

    @subsubsection readonly_function_parameters Read-Only Function Parameters
    A parameter can be prefixed with \c const to make the parameter binding read-only inside the function, method, constructor, closure, or catch block where it is declared:
    @code{.py}
%modern

int sub clamp(const int value, const int min_value, const int max_value) {
    if (value < min_value) {
        return min_value;
    }
    if (value > max_value) {
        return max_value;
    }
    # value = 0;  # READONLY-VARIABLE-ASSIGNMENT-ERROR
    return value;
}

sub handle(const auto payload, const *string request_id = NOTHING) {
    # request_id = "new";  # READONLY-VARIABLE-ASSIGNMENT-ERROR
}
    @endcode

    Read-only parameters are still passed by value or by reference according to the normal call rules; \c const only prevents writes through the parameter name in the function body. It is not part of overload resolution, so \c "sub f(int x)" and \c "sub f(const int x)" have the same parameter type signature.

    As with @ref readonly_local_variables "read-only local variables", the restriction is shallow: it protects the binding and lvalue paths rooted at that parameter, but it does not make an object or container globally immutable.

    Variables passed as function arguments are passed by value by default, unless the caller places a
    <tt>&quot;\\&quot;</tt> character before an lvalue in the argument list in the function call. In this case the
    function must have a parameter defined to accept the variable passed by reference. Any changes to the local
    variable will be reflected in the original variable for variables passed by reference. Also note that it is
    illegal to pass a local variable by reference in a @ref background "background" expression.

    @note In order to process a variable number of arguments to a function, use @ref implicit_arguments "implicit argument references" (<tt>$1</tt>) or the <tt>argv</tt> variable (an automatic local variable); these are automatically instantiated at run time when additional arguments in excess of the declared parameters are passed to the function at run time.  No declaration needs to be made in the function signature to use the <tt>argv</tt> variable.

    @subsubsection function_param_varargs Ellipses For Variable Arguments

    Ellipses (\c "...") may be provided as the only or last parameter, indicating the the function takes a variable
    number of arguments.

    This is most important with @ref abstract "abstract" class methods.

    @subsection function_type_parameters Function Type Parameters
    Functions can declare formal type parameters after the function name. The
    type parameters can then be used in the return type, parameter types, local
    declarations, and nested type declarations in the function body:

    @code{.py}
T sub identity<T>(T value) {
    T local = value;
    return local;
}

int i = identity(1);                         # T inferred as int
string s = identity(value: "ready");         # named calls also infer T
string explicit = identity<string>("done");  # explicit call type argument
    @endcode

    Explicit generic call type arguments are written after the function name
    and before the normal argument list. Explicit type arguments take precedence
    over inference and are required when the call has no arguments from which a
    type parameter can be inferred:

    @code{.py}
T sub make<T = int>() {
    auto value = 1;
    T rv = value;
    return rv;
}

int default_value = make();          # default type argument T = int
int explicit_value = make<int>();    # explicit T
    @endcode

    Type parameters can declare an upper bound and a default. Bounds are
    checked after inference or after explicit type arguments are applied:

    @code{.py}
T sub bounded<T: int = int>(T value = 1) {
    return value;
}

int ok = bounded(2);
int explicit_ok = bounded<int>(3);
# string bad = bounded<string>("x");  # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR
    @endcode

    Methods and static methods use the same call-type-argument syntax:

    @code{.py}
class Chooser {
    T echo<T>(T value) {
        return value;
    }

    static U choose<T, U>(T left, U right) {
        return right;
    }
}

Chooser c();
int method_value = c.echo(4);
string explicit_method_value = c.echo<string>(value: "four");
string static_value = Chooser::choose<int, string>(1, "one");
    @endcode

    If inference cannot choose one unique type argument, or if an explicit type
    argument violates a bound, parsing raises \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR. Functions
    or methods without type parameters cannot be called with generic call type
    arguments.

    @subsection function_named_arguments Named Arguments
    Function, method, constructor, and base-constructor calls can pass arguments by parameter name using
    <tt>name: expression</tt> syntax. Named arguments make call sites clearer when a call has optional arguments,
    boolean switches, timeouts, encodings, or several arguments with related types:

    @code{.py}
sub connect(string host, int port = 443, *int timeout = NOTHING, bool tls = True) {
    # ...
}

connect(host: "example.com");
connect("example.com", timeout: 30, tls: False);
connect(host: "example.com", tls: False, timeout: 10);
    @endcode

    Positional arguments may appear before named arguments, but a positional argument cannot follow a named
    argument in the same call. Named arguments are matched case-sensitively against the selected variant's declared
    parameter names. Duplicate names, unknown names, or names that would overwrite a parameter already filled by a
    positional argument are parse-time errors.

    Parameters may be supplied in any order by name. Parameters omitted from a named call use the same
    default-argument handling as positional calls:

    @code{.py}
sub resize(string source, string target, int width = 1024, int height = 768, bool keep_aspect = True) {
    # ...
}

resize(source: "in.png", target: "out.png", height: 480);
resize("in.png", "thumb.png", keep_aspect: False, width: 128, height: 128);
    @endcode

    This applies whatever the declared type of the omitted parameter: an omitted \c auto or untyped parameter
    without a default receives @ref nothing, like an omitted or-nothing parameter. Omitting a parameter that neither
    accepts @ref nothing nor has a default is a \c PARSE-TYPE-ERROR naming that parameter:

    @code{.py}
sub send(string target, auto payload, int timeout = 30) {
    # ...
}

send("queue-1", timeout: 5);  # payload is NOTHING
    @endcode

    Named arguments bind to fixed parameters only; they do not bind to varargs or the \c argv variable.

    @code{.py}
sub log(string level, string message, ...) {
    # level and message can be named; varargs cannot
}

log(level: "INFO", message: "started");
log("INFO", "started", "request_id", 123);  # extra values remain positional
    @endcode

    Argument expressions are evaluated in source order, even when the resulting values are assigned to parameters in
    a different order:

    @code{.py}
sub pair(int a, int b) {
    printf("%d:%d\n", a, b);
}

pair(b: side_effect_1(), a: side_effect_2());
    @endcode

    In the example above, \c side_effect_1() is evaluated before \c side_effect_2(), but the resulting call still
    passes the second value as \c a and the first value as \c b.

    Named arguments also work with methods, constructors, and base constructors when the target signature is known at
    parse time:

    @code{.py}
class Reader {
    constructor(string path, string encoding = "UTF-8", bool strict = True) {
        # ...
    }

    string readLine(int max_chars = 4096, bool trim = True) {
        # ...
    }
}

class CsvReader inherits Reader {
    constructor(string path, string encoding = "UTF-8")
        : Reader(path: path, encoding: encoding, strict: False) {
    }
}

Reader r(path: "data.csv", encoding: "UTF-8");
string line = r.readLine(trim: False);
    @endcode

    Builtin functions and methods can support named arguments as well. For example:

    @code{.py}
string decoded = binary_to_string(value: body, encoding: "UTF-8");
date ts = localtime(seconds: epoch_seconds, microseconds: 250000);
binary frame = ws_encode_frame(message: payload, opcode: WSOP_Text, masked: False, fin: True);
    @endcode

    Some builtin variants intentionally remain positional-only, for example runtime-dispatched, varargs-only, or
    compatibility-sensitive variants. Such calls produce a named-call diagnostic explaining why the call is not
    supported and, when possible, which named parameters are available on named-callable variants.

    A call with named arguments must resolve to a known signature at parse time. Calls through untyped call
    references or closures, and method calls through a receiver without a declared class type (such as \c auto,
    \c object, or a class with a \c methodGate() method and no method of the given name), are rejected with
    \c NAMED-CALL-NOT-SUPPORTED; use positional arguments or give the target an explicit type that allows parse-time
    resolution. A method call through a receiver declared with an or-nothing class type (<tt>*Class</tt>) binds the
    named arguments with the signature of the method in \c Class; the method is still dispatched at runtime. Builtin functions and methods accept named arguments only
    when their variant has opted in with the @ref NAMED_ARGS "NAMED_ARGS" code flag.

    When the target has a single variant, an argument whose type is only known at runtime (for example an \c auto
    value, or a <tt>*string</tt> value for an \c auto parameter) is bound by name at parse time and type-checked at
    runtime exactly as the same positional argument would be. When the target is overloaded, the arguments are bound
    to the parameter names of a variant at parse time; when the variant depends on an argument's runtime type (or
    value, as for an argument that can have no value, see @ref overloading), it is resolved at runtime among the
    variants that have the same parameter names at the positions of the arguments, so the arguments are never passed
    to a variant by position.  If another variant that the runtime resolution could select has other parameter names
    at those positions, the call is rejected with \c NAMED-CALL-NOT-SUPPORTED; use positional arguments or give the
    argument an explicit type that has a value.

    @note Parameter names are part of the public calling interface for named callers. Renaming a public parameter can
    break callers that use named arguments even when positional callers are unaffected.

    @note At a call site, <tt>f(a: 1)</tt> is a named-argument call. To pass a paren-form hash as one positional
    argument, keep the extra parentheses: <tt>f((a: 1))</tt>.

    @subsection function_return_types Function Return Type Declarations
    The return type of the function can be given by placing a type declaration before the <tt><b>sub</b></tt> keyword (the older syntax with the <tt><b>returns</b></tt> keyword after the parameter list is still accepted as well).

    @note Parameter and return types are required when the @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_PROTOTYPES parse options are set.
    @note If the return type is missing, it is assumed to be of type @ref auto_type "auto" (not @ref any_type "any", which would strip complex type information). In case @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_PROTOTYPES parse options are set, and the return type is missing from function's declaration, it is assumed to be of type @ref nothing.

    Functions use the @ref return "return statement" to provide a return value to the caller.

    @subsection function_examples Simple Example Functions

    Here is an example function declaration returning a value:
    @code{.py}
#!/usr/bin/qore
# function declaration example
%new-style

int sub print_string(string string) {
    print("%s\n", string);
    return 1;
}@endcode

    Functions may also be recursive. Here is an example of a recursive %Qore function implementing the Fibonacci function:
    @code{.py}
#!/usr/bin/qore
#
# recursive function example
%new-style

int sub fibonacci(int num) {
    if (num == 1)
        return 1;
    return num * fibonacci(num - 1);
}@endcode

    @note Function names are resolved during the second parse pass; therefore functions do not need to be declared before being referenced. This allows an easy definition of 2 or more self-referencing functions.

    @subsection synchronized_functions "Synchronized" Functions
    Functions declared with the <tt><b>synchronized</b></tt> keyword will only run in one thread at a time.

    If another thread tries to call the function while the function is already being executed, any callers will block until the function has finished executing.

    Note that the lock used is recursive, so that a single thread may call the function multiple times safely without fear of a deadlock.  The lock used also participates in %Qore's deadlock detection framework, therefore if a deadlock is detected, a \c THREAD-DEADLOCK exception is thrown.

    @see @ref synchronized

    @subsection deprecated-functions "Deprecated" Functions
    Functions declared with the @ref deprecated keyword will cause a @ref deprecated-warning "deprecated warning" to be raised when the function is referenced (but not when it's declared).

    In this way API functions (or methods) can be declared as @ref deprecated before eventual removal from the API set.

    @subsection function_domains Functional Domains of Functions and Methods
    A function or method declares the @ref sandbox_parse_options "functional domains" that it provides with an
    attribute list after its parameter list, as builtin functions and methods are declared in their sources: code that
    a @ref Qore::Program "Program" may not use because of a restriction in its parse options (such as
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL "PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL") is then refused to that %Program, as builtin code is.

    @par Attribute List Syntax
    <tt>[dom=</tt><em>domain</em><tt>[,</tt><em>domain</em> ...<tt>][;]]</tt>

    The domains are given without the \c QDOM_ prefix, in upper or lower case, separated by commas; elements of the
    list are separated by semicolons, and a trailing semicolon is allowed.  The only key is \c dom; an unknown key or
    an unknown domain is a parse error.  The names are those of the \c DOM_* constants, which the
    reflection module also gives (\c getDomainStringList()); \c PROCESS and \c MODULES, the names that
    the sources of builtin code use, are accepted for \c PROCESS_CONTROL and \c MODULE.  The domains and the parse
    options that restrict them:
    |!Domain|!Restricted by
    |\c PROCESS_CONTROL (or \c PROCESS)|@ref Qore::PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL
    |\c NETWORK|@ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK
    |\c EXTERNAL_PROCESS|@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS
    |\c FILESYSTEM|@ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM
    |\c THREAD_CLASS|@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CLASSES
    |\c THREAD_CONTROL|@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL
    |\c DATABASE|@ref Qore::PO_NO_DATABASE
    |\c GUI|@ref Qore::PO_NO_GUI
    |\c TERMINAL_IO|@ref Qore::PO_NO_TERMINAL_IO
    |\c EXTERNAL_INFO|@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_INFO
    |\c THREAD_INFO|@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_INFO
    |\c LOCALE_CONTROL|@ref Qore::PO_NO_LOCALE_CONTROL
    |\c MODULE (or \c MODULES)|@ref Qore::PO_NO_MODULES
    |\c IN_MODULE|@ref Qore::PO_IN_MODULE
    |\c EMBEDDED_LOGIC|@ref Qore::PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC
    |\c REFLECTION|@ref Qore::PO_NO_REFLECTION
    |\c UNCONTROLLED_API|@ref Qore::PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS
    |\c INJECTION|requires @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_INJECTION
    |\c DEBUGGER|requires @ref Qore::PO_ALLOW_DEBUGGER

    The attribute list follows the parameter list and the @ref const_methods "const" qualifier, and precedes a base
    class constructor list and the deprecated \c returns clause:
    @code{.py}
%modern

# a function that changes the state of the whole process
public sub register_handler(string name, code handler) [dom=PROCESS_CONTROL] {
    ...
}

# a function that uses the network and the filesystem
public string sub fetch_to_file(string url, string path) [dom=NETWORK,FILESYSTEM] {
    ...
}

public class Registry {
    # static, abstract and const methods, constructors
    static setLogger(*LoggerInterface logger) [dom=PROCESS_CONTROL] { ... }
    abstract register(string name) [dom=PROCESS_CONTROL];
    int count() const [dom=PROCESS_CONTROL] { ... }
    constructor(string name) [dom=PROCESS_CONTROL] : Base(name) { ... }
}
    @endcode

    Destructors, \c copy(), \c methodGate(), \c memberGate() and \c memberNotification() methods, which run without
    being named, and @ref closure "closures" cannot declare functional domains.

    A %Program whose parse options restrict a domain:
    - cannot reference code that declares it: a call of the function or method (including a constructor called with
      @ref new "new" or a variable declaration, and a base class constructor list), and a
      @ref call_reference "call reference" to it, raise a \c PARSE-EXCEPTION naming the domain and the parse option
      that restricts it.  A call of an overloaded function whose variant cannot be chosen when the code is parsed is
      checked when the variant is chosen at run time
    - is refused a call made at run time: a method call on an object whose class is not known when the code is parsed,
      including a call that dispatches to an override in a subclass, @ref Qore::call_function() "call_function()",
      @ref Qore::call_object_method() "call_object_method()", @ref Qore::create_object() "create_object()", a base
      class constructor run for a subclass without a base class constructor list, and the reflection API raise an
      \c INVALID-FUNCTION-ACCESS exception

    A call reference or a @ref closure "closure" runs with the permissions of the context that created it: a call
    reference to code with a functional domain that code allowed to use the domain created can be called by code
    that is not allowed to use it.  A closure created in code that declares a functional domain does not inherit the
    domain.

    @par Overrides
    A method that overrides a method declaring functional domains must declare every one of them; a method that does
    not raises a \c DOMAIN-METHOD-ERROR parse exception.  An override may declare more functional domains than the
    method it overrides; a call that dispatches to it at run time is then checked against them.  Static methods do not
    override.

    @par Functional Domains and User Modules
    The code of a @ref user_modules "user module" runs in the module's own @ref Qore::Program "Program", which has the
    restrictions of the %Program that loads the module, so module code that uses a functional domain that the loading
    %Program may not use cannot be loaded by it.  A module that uses functional domains itself, such as a module that
    registers process-wide functionality when it is loaded, declares them with the \c domains key of its
    @ref user_module_declarations "module declaration": its %Program then does not have the restrictions of those
    domains, if the module is found in a directory of the process-wide module path (see
    @ref user_module_declarations).  The code that calls the module is still checked against its own restrictions.

    @since %Qore 3.0
*/
